WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.520
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:11.059
This day is the perfect day to simulate the
kinematics of yarn and cloth on our computers.

00:00:11.059 --> 00:00:16.909
As you just saw, this is not the usual intro
that we use in every episode, so, what could

00:00:16.909 --> 00:00:17.970
that be?

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:24.349
Well, this is a simulation specifically made
for us, using a technique from today’s paper.

00:00:24.349 --> 00:00:29.340
And it has a super high stitching density,
which makes it all the better, by the end

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:34.179
of this video, you will know exactly what
that means and why that matters.

00:00:34.179 --> 00:00:39.910
But first, for context I would like to show
you what researchers were able to do in 2012

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:44.390
and we will see together how far we have come
since.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:49.370
This previous work was about creating these
highly detailed cloth geometries for digital

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:50.370
characters.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:56.539
Here you see one of its coolest results where
it shows how the simulated forces pull the

00:00:56.539 --> 00:00:59.749
entire piece of garment together.

00:00:59.749 --> 00:01:05.560
We start out by dreaming up a piece of cloth
geometry, and this simulator gradually transforms

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:11.549
it into a real-world version of that by subjecting
it to real physical forces.

00:01:11.549 --> 00:01:14.750
This is a step that we call yarn-level relaxation.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:22.189
So, this paper was published in 2012, and
now, nearly 9 years have passed, so I wonder

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:24.640
how far have we come some since?

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.670
Well, we can still simulate knitted and woven
materials through similar programs that we

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.860
call direct yarn-level simulations.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:34.049
Here’s one.

00:01:34.049 --> 00:01:41.369
I think we can all agree that these are absolutely
beautiful, so what’s the catch?

00:01:41.369 --> 00:01:46.689
The catch is that this is not free, there
is a price we have to pay for these results.

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:47.899
Look.

00:01:47.899 --> 00:01:51.220
Whoa, these really take forever.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:57.640
We are talking several hours, or for this
one, almost even an entire day to simulate

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.289
just one piece of garment.

00:02:00.289 --> 00:02:06.100
And it gets worse, look, this one takes more
than two full days to compute.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:11.110
Imagine how long we would have to wait for
complex scenes in a feature-length movie with

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:12.600
several characters.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:18.680
Now, of course, this problem is very challenging,
and to solve it, we have to perform a direct

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.760
yarn-level simulation.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:25.990
This means that every single strand of yarn
is treated as an elastic rod, and we have

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:31.900
to compute how they react to external forces,
bending deformations, and more.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.170
That takes a great deal of computation.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:39.610
So, our question today is, can we do this
in a more reasonable amount of time?

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:44.080
Well, the first Law Of Papers says that research
is a process.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:49.480
Do not look at where we are, look at where
we will be two more papers down the line.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.100
Let’s see if the law holds up here!

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:58.780
This new paper promises to retain many important
characteristics of the full simulation, but

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.610
takes much less time to compute.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:02.950
That is amazing.

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:11.140
Things stretch, bend, and even curl up similarly,
but the simulation time is cut down a great

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:12.350
deal.

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:13.530
How much less?

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:18.790
Well, this one is five times faster, that’s
great.

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:22.680
This one, twenty times, oh my!

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:23.960
But it gets better!

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.050
Now, hold on to your papers, and look!

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:32.300
This one is almost 60 times faster than the
full yarn-level simulation.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:34.150
My goodness!

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:40.000
However, of course, it’s not perfect, in
return, pulling effects on individual yarns

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.300
is neglected, so we lose the look of this
amazing holey geometry.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.840
I’d love to get that back.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:52.410
Now, these examples were using materials with
a relatively small stitching density.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:58.100
Now, the previous, full yarn-level simulation
method scales with the number of yarn segments

00:03:58.100 --> 00:03:59.500
we add to the garment.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:02.440
So, what does all this mean?

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:08.120
This means that higher stitching density gives
us more yarn strands, and the more yarn strands

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:11.490
there are, the longer it takes to simulate
them.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:16.829
In these cases, you can see the knitting patterns,
so there aren’t that many yarns, and even

00:04:16.829 --> 00:04:23.069
with that, it still took multiple days to
compute a simulation with one piece of garment.

00:04:23.069 --> 00:04:25.669
So, I hope you know what’s coming.

00:04:25.669 --> 00:04:30.830
It can also simulate super high stitching
densities efficiently.

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:31.860
What does that mean?

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:37.599
It means that is can also simulate materials
like the satin example here.

00:04:37.599 --> 00:04:43.259
This is not bad by any means, but similar
simulations can be done with much simpler

00:04:43.259 --> 00:04:46.650
simulators, so our question is, why does this
matter?

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:53.050
Well, let’s look at the backside here, and
marvel at this beautiful scene showcasing

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:56.420
the second best curl of the day.

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:57.570
Loving it.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:01.340
And now I hope you are wondering what the
best curl of the day is.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:08.270
Yes, here goes, this is the one that was showcased
in our intro, which is Two Minute Papers curling

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:09.270
up.

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.740
You can only see the footage here.

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:13.069
Beautiful.

00:05:13.069 --> 00:05:18.310
Huge congratulations and a big thank you to
Georg Sperl, the first author of this paper,

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:23.819
who simulated this Two Minute Papers scene
only for us, and with a super high stitching

00:05:23.819 --> 00:05:24.819
density.

00:05:24.819 --> 00:05:26.450
That is quite an honor.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:27.450
Thank you so much!

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:34.600
As I promised, we now understand exactly why
the stitching density makes it all the better.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:38.479
And if you have been watching this series
for a while, I am sure you have seen computer

00:05:38.479 --> 00:05:43.960
graphics researchers destroy armadillos in
the most spectacular manner.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.830
Make sure to leave a comment if this is the
case.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:52.860
That is super fun, and I thought there must
be a version of that for cloth simulations.

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:54.349
And of course there is.

00:05:54.349 --> 00:05:57.669
Now, please please meet the Yarnmadillo.

00:05:57.669 --> 00:06:00.689
The naming game is very strong here.

00:06:00.689 --> 00:06:02.430
And just one more thing.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:08.220
Georg Sperl, the first author of this work
was a student of mine in 2014 at the Technical

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:13.840
University of Vienna where he finished a practical
project in light simulations programs, and,

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:16.189
he did excellent work there.

00:06:16.189 --> 00:06:17.460
Of course he did.

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:22.580
And I will note that I was not part of this
project in any way, I am just super happy

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:28.759
to see him come so far since then, he is now
nearing the completion of his PhD, and this

00:06:28.759 --> 00:06:32.830
cloth simulation paper of his was accepted
to the SIGGRAPH conference.

00:06:32.830 --> 00:07:11.279
That is as good 

00:07:11.279 --> 00:07:27.380
as it gets - well done Georg!

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:35.479
Thanks 
for watching and for your generous support,

00:07:35.479 --> 00:07:35.589
and I'll see you next time!

